{
  "id": "ugly-number-ii",
  "questionId": "ugly-number-ii",
  "slug": "ugly-number-ii",
  "title": "Ugly Number II",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Heap",
  "patterns": [
    "heap",
    "min-heap",
    "number theory"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "instead of testing every integer for ugliness, can you directly generate ugly numbers in increasing order?",
    "every ugly number besides 1 is some smaller ugly number multiplied by 2, 3, or 5 - so the sequence can be built outward from 1",
    "use a min-heap seeded with 1. repeatedly pop the smallest value, and push its products with 2, 3, and 5, skipping any value you've already generated to avoid duplicates (like 2*3 and 3*2 producing 6 twice)",
    "the nth value popped from the heap is your answer - track a seen set (or check against the last popped value) to avoid processing the same ugly number more than once"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "not deduplicating, so the same ugly number (like 6, reachable as 2*3 or 3*2) gets counted multiple times and throws off the count",
    "starting the heap empty instead of seeded with 1, missing the first ugly number entirely",
    "confusing 'nth ugly number' (1-indexed) with a 0-indexed position, off-by-one in the loop bound"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "An ugly number is a positive integer whose only prime factors are 2, 3, and 5. You're given a number `n`.\n\n**Return the `n`th ugly number in ascending order.**\n\nNumbering is 1-indexed, so n = 1 returns 1. 1 counts as ugly, since it has no prime factors at all.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= n <= 1690",
      "1 is considered the first ugly number"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "n = 1, the smallest possible input",
      "n large enough that the result exceeds the count of small ugly numbers you might enumerate by hand"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
